tel 1.0

base charisma
import soundness

info   the periodic table, molecules, formulas and chemical equations as typed values
detail
    _Charisma_ models chemical notation as structure rather than text. The 118 elements are
    values on `PeriodicTable`; elements bond into a `Molecule` with `*`, which can carry a
    charge and a physical state; molecules with coefficients sum into a
    `Chemical.Formula` with `+`; and two formulas join, with one of five typed reaction
    arrows, into a `Chemical.Equation` which can say whether it balances.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import PeriodicTable.{H, O}

    val water = H[2]*O
    water.show                                  // t"H₂O"

    val synthesis = H[2]*2 + O[2] --> water*2
    synthesis.show                              // t"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"
    synthesis.balanced                          // true
    ```

    The operators are defined on two traits: `Molecular`, which an element and a molecule
    extend, has the bond `*`, the coefficient `*` and the charge operators; `Formulable`,
    which every molecular value and every formula extends, has `+` and the reaction arrows.
    So an element, a molecule and a formula can all stand on either side of an equation.

    Each value renders with `show` in conventional notation — subscript counts,
    superscript charges, the Hill order with carbon and hydrogen first — and inspects
    distinctly, so that an element, a molecule and a one-term formula are never confused in
    a test failure.

    The library is a single source set, `charisma.core`, which is platform-neutral, and
    everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. It has no givens to import.

refer charisma.PeriodicTable
refer charisma.Molecule
refer charisma.Chemical
refer charisma.Molecular
refer charisma.Formulable

entry .Chemical   a namespace for the chemistry vocabulary: elements, formulas and equations
  detail
      There is no `Chemical` type: the object holds `Element`, `Formula` and `Equation`, the
      three kinds of chemical thing whose names would otherwise be too general for the
      umbrella. Molecules are `Molecule`, at the package's top level.
  refer charisma.PeriodicTable
  refer charisma.Molecule
  entry .Element   a chemical element: its atomic number, symbol and name
    detail
        An element is a value, usually taken from `PeriodicTable`, and is `Molecular`: on its
        own it is a molecule of one atom, it bonds with `*`, and a type argument repeats it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import PeriodicTable.{O, Fe}

        Fe.number      // 26
        Fe.show        // t"Fe"
        O[2].show      // t"O₂"
        Fe.inspect     // t"⚛Fe(26)"
        ```

        An element shows as its bare symbol, and inspects with an atom sign and its atomic
        number, which tells it apart from any other short text.
    entry .showable   shows an element as its symbol, `Fe`
    entry .inspectable   inspects an element with an atom sign and its number, `⚛Fe(26)`
    entry #number   the atomic number
    entry #symbol   the chemical symbol, `Fe`
    entry #name   the English name, `Iron`
    entry #apply   a molecule of this element repeated, with the count as a type argument, `O[2]`
        `O[2]` is the molecule of two oxygen atoms: the count is a literal natural-number type,
        so it must be known as the code compiles. For a count known only at runtime, use
        `O ** n`.
    entry #molecule   this element as a molecule of one atom

  entry .Formula   a sum of molecules, each with a coefficient: `2H₂ + O₂`
    detail
        A formula is a `Ledger` from each `Molecule` to its coefficient. It is made from a
        molecular value with a coefficient (`H[2]*2`) or with `.apply`, and grows with `+`,
        which adds the coefficients of a molecule that appears in both terms.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import PeriodicTable.{H, O}

        val reactants = H[2]*2 + O[2]
        reactants.show       // t"2H₂ + O₂"
        reactants.atoms      // Map(H -> 4, O -> 2)
        reactants.inspect    // t"2H₂ + 1O₂"
        ```

        A coefficient of 1 is left implicit by `show` but written out by `inspect`, so a
        one-term formula never inspects the same as the molecule it holds.
    entry .apply   a formula of a single molecule with a coefficient of 1
    entry .showable   shows a formula as its terms joined by ` + `, omitting coefficients of 1
    entry .inspectable   inspects a formula with every coefficient written, or `∅` if it is empty
    entry #molecules   each molecule of the formula, with its coefficient
    entry #formula   the formula itself, as `Formulable` requires
    entry #atoms   the number of atoms of each element across every term, counting coefficients
        The multiset of atoms on one side of an equation: each molecule's atoms multiplied by
        its coefficient, summed. Two sides balance when their `atoms` are equal.

  entry .Equation   two formulas joined by a reaction arrow: `2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O`
    detail
        An equation is made from two formulable values with one of `Formulable`'s arrows,
        `-->`, `<->`, `<=>`, `<~>` or `===`, each giving a different `Reaction`.

        ```scala fuel
        alternative Forward
          code
              import soundness.*
              import PeriodicTable.{H, O}

              (H[2]*2 + O[2] --> (H[2]*O)*2).show   // t"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"
        alternative Both directions
          code
              import soundness.*
              import PeriodicTable.{H, O}

              (H[2]*2 + O[2] <=> (H[2]*O)*2).show   // t"2H₂ + O₂ ⇄ 2H₂O"
        alternative Equilibrium
          code
              import soundness.*
              import PeriodicTable.{H, O}

              (H[2]*2 + O[2] <~> (H[2]*O)*2).show   // t"2H₂ + O₂ ⇋ 2H₂O"
        ##
        ```

        `#balanced` compares the atoms of the two sides; nothing else is checked, so an
        unbalanced equation is a valid value.
    entry .showable   shows an equation as its two sides with the reaction's arrow between them
    entry .inspectable   inspects an equation with its parts and the reaction named, not drawn
        `Equation(2H₂ + 1O₂ ╱ NetForward ╱ 2H₂O)`, so that its inspection is never the
        notation `show` produces.
    entry #lhs   the formula on the left of the arrow
    entry #reaction   the kind of reaction the arrow denotes
    entry #rhs   the formula on the right of the arrow
    entry #balanced   whether both sides have the same number of atoms of each element
        Compares `lhs.atoms` with `rhs.atoms`. Charge is not compared, so an equation whose
        sides differ only in their charge counts as balanced.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import PeriodicTable.{H, O}

        (H[2]*2 + O[2] --> (H[2]*O)*2).balanced   // true
        (H[2] + O[2] --> H[2]*O).balanced         // false
        ```

entry .Molecule   atoms bonded together, with an electric charge and an optional physical state
  detail
      A molecule is a `Map` from each element to its count, a charge, and an optional
      `PhysicalState`. It is usually built from elements with `Molecular`'s operators rather
      than constructed directly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import PeriodicTable.{C, Cl, H, O, S}

      (C[6]*H[12]*O[6]).show                          // t"C₆H₁₂O₆"
      (S*O[4]).ion(-2).show                           // t"O₄S²⁻"
      (-Cl.molecule).inState(PhysicalState.Aqueous).show  // t"Cl⁻(aq)"
      ```

      It shows in Hill order: carbon first and then hydrogen when the molecule has carbon,
      and every other element (hydrogen included, in a molecule without carbon)
      alphabetically by symbol. Counts are subscripts, a charge is a superscript magnitude
      (omitted for ±1) followed by `⁺` or `⁻`, and the state follows in parentheses.
      Bonding two molecules sums their atoms and charges and leaves the state unset, so a
      state must be applied to the finished molecule.
  refer charisma.Molecular
  refer charisma.PeriodicTable
  entry .apply   an empty, uncharged molecule, or one of a single atom of an element
  entry .showable   shows a molecule in Hill order, with subscript counts, charge and state: `O₄S²⁻(aq)`
  entry .inspectable   inspects a molecule as the formula `show` gives, `H₂O`
  entry #elements   each element of the molecule, with its number of atoms
  entry #charge   the electric charge, in units of the elementary charge
  entry #state   the physical state, or `Unset` if none was given
  entry #molecule   the molecule itself, as `Molecular` requires

entry .Molecular   anything which is a molecule: bonds with `*`, scales, and carries a charge and state
  detail
      A trait extended by `Chemical.Element` and `Molecule`. Each operator works on the
      value's `#molecule`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import PeriodicTable.{Cl, H, N, Na, O}

      (H[2]*O).show          // t"H₂O": a bond
      (H[2]*O ** 3).show     // t"H₆O₃": every atom tripled, one molecule
      (H[2]*O*2).show        // t"2H₂O": a coefficient, a formula
      (+(N*H[4])).show       // t"H₄N⁺"
      (+Na*(-Cl)).charge     // 0
      ```

      There are two multiplications, both spelt `*`: by another molecular value it bonds
      them into one `Molecule`, and by an `Int` it gives a `Chemical.Formula` with that
      coefficient. `**` multiplies the atoms within the molecule instead.
  refer charisma.Molecule
  refer charisma.Formulable
  entry #molecule   this value as a `Molecule`
  entry #formula   this value as a formula of one molecule with a coefficient of 1
  entry #*   bonds with another molecule, or gives a formula with an `Int` coefficient
      `H[2]*O` is one molecule with the atoms and charges of both; `water*2` is the formula
      `2H₂O`. The bond leaves the physical state unset.
  entry #**   the molecule with every atom count and its charge multiplied
      `(H[2]*O) ** 3` is `H₆O₃`, a single molecule; compare `*` with an `Int`, which gives a
      coefficient.
  entry #unary_-   the molecule with its charge lowered by one: `-Cl` is the chloride ion
  entry #unary_+   the molecule with its charge raised by one: `+Na` is the sodium ion
  entry #ion   the molecule with its charge replaced: `Fe.ion(3)`
  entry #inState   the molecule with its physical state replaced, or removed with `Unset`
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import PeriodicTable.{Cl, Na}

      (Na*Cl).inState(PhysicalState.Aqueous).show   // t"ClNa(aq)"
      ```

      Mind the precedence: `Na*Cl.inState(…)` sets the state on the chlorine alone, and the
      bond then discards it.

entry .Formulable   anything which can be one side of an equation: adds with `+` and takes a reaction arrow
  detail
      A trait extended by `Molecular` values and by `Chemical.Formula`. Its operators work on
      the value's `#formula`, so elements, molecules and formulas mix freely.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import PeriodicTable.{C, H, O}

      val glucose = C[6]*H[12]*O[6]
      val combustion = glucose + O[2]*6 --> (C*O[2]*6 + H[2]*O*6)
      combustion.balanced   // true
      ```

      `-->` begins with `-`, so it binds exactly as tightly as `+` and associates to the
      left: a sum on its right must be parenthesised, or the `+` applies to the equation
      and the line does not compile. The other arrows bind more loosely than `+`.

      Each arrow makes a `Chemical.Equation` with a different `Reaction`: `-->` a net forward
      reaction, `<=>` one proceeding in both directions, `<~>` one at equilibrium, `<->` a
      resonance, and `===` a stoichiometric relation.
  refer charisma.Chemical.Equation
  refer charisma.Reaction
  entry #formula   this value as a `Chemical.Formula`
  entry #+   the sum of two formulas, adding the coefficients of a molecule in both
  entry #-->   an equation with a net forward reaction, `→`
      `lhs --> rhs`. It binds as tightly as `+` and associates left, so a sum on the right
      needs parentheses: `CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O` is
      `C*H[4] + O[2]*2 --> (C*O[2] + H[2]*O*2)`.
  entry #<->   an equation with a resonance, `Reaction.Resonance`
  entry #<=>   an equation proceeding in both directions, `⇄`
  entry #<~>   an equation at equilibrium, `⇋`
  entry #===   an equation with a stoichiometric relation, `Reaction.Stoichiometric`

entry .PhysicalState   the state of matter of a molecule: solid, liquid, gas or aqueous
  detail
      An enumeration of `Solid`, `Liquid`, `Gas` and `Aqueous`, shown as `(s)`, `(l)`, `(g)`
      and `(aq)` after a molecule. A state is applied with `Molecular#inState` and is an
      annotation, not part of the substance's identity for bonding: bonding two molecules
      leaves the result's state unset.
  entry .showable   shows a state as `(s)`, `(l)`, `(g)` or `(aq)`

entry .Reaction   the relation a chemical equation asserts between its two sides
  detail
      An enumeration of `NetForward` (shown `→`, from `-->`), `BothDirections` (`⇄`, from
      `<=>`), `Equilibrium` (`⇋`, from `<~>`), `Stoichiometric` (`↔`, from `===`) and
      `Resonance` (`=`, from `<->`). Which arrow a chemist writes carries meaning, and an
      equation keeps it.
  entry .showable   shows a reaction as its arrow

entry .PeriodicTable   the 118 chemical elements, by symbol, atomic number or name
  detail
      Every element is a `val` named by its symbol, so they are imported individually or all
      at once; and an element can be found at runtime by its number or its symbol.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import PeriodicTable.{Fe, H}

      PeriodicTable(26)         // Fe
      PeriodicTable(t"Cu")      // Cu
      PeriodicTable(t"cu")      // Unset: symbols are case-sensitive
      PeriodicTable(119)        // Unset
      PeriodicTable.element(119).name   // t"Ununennium"
      ```

      Beyond oganesson, `.element` gives the IUPAC systematic element for any atomic number.
  refer charisma.Chemical.Element
  entry .apply   the element with an atomic number or a symbol, or `Unset` if there is none
      `PeriodicTable(26)` and `PeriodicTable(t"Fe")` are both iron; a number outside 1 to 118
      or an unknown symbol is `Unset`. Symbol lookup is case-sensitive.
  entry .elements   every element, in order of atomic number
  entry .symbols   every element, keyed by its symbol
  entry .element   the element with an atomic number, with its IUPAC systematic name and symbol
      Builds the placeholder name from the digits of the number — `nil`, `un`, `bi`, `tri`,
      `quad`, `pent`, `hex`, `sept`, `oct`, `enn` — and `ium`, with a doubled `i` elided:
      119 is `Ununennium` (`Uue`), 120 `Unbinilium` (`Ubn`), and 122 `Unbibium` (`Ubb`). It
      gives a systematic element for every number, including those which have a real name
      in the table.
  entry .H   hydrogen, element 1
  entry .He   helium, element 2
  entry .Li   lithium, element 3
  entry .Be   beryllium, element 4
  entry .B   boron, element 5
  entry .C   carbon, element 6
  entry .N   nitrogen, element 7
  entry .O   oxygen, element 8
  entry .F   fluorine, element 9
  entry .Ne   neon, element 10
  entry .Na   sodium, element 11
